0; itself, adds or subtracts header numbers, or shows an end screen while an   answer is pending or a card is held. Screens: &#x60;card&#x60;; &#x60;settling&#x60; (no card held but one   is expected: keep the header, show no grade buttons and no Refresh-only state, fetch   at once and again whenever a pending answer is acknowledged; in a browser that is   offline say \&quot;saving your answers\&quot; after 10 s); &#x60;waiting&#x60;, &#x60;goal_reached&#x60;,   &#x60;done_for_today&#x60;, &#x60;empty&#x60; as in C4. On those four the Keep going button is   &#x60;loop.keep_going_available&#x60; and the countdown &#x60;loop.later_today.next_due_at&#x60; of the   same &#x60;state&#x60;. Home, widgets and the CLI status line have no pending answers and no   held card; for them the presenter returns &#x60;loop.until_stop_parts&#x60; and the phase   unchanged, so C3 holds as written. The CLI may keep rendering &#x60;loop&#x60; directly (it   answers synchronously and holds no page). - **C8 Persisted outbox (C-31, ADR-050; browsers without a local replica, i.e. desktop   web; not the shell, where an answer is applied locally before its response).** A   grade tap never waits for the network. The client (1) writes the   &#x60;ReviewAnswerRequest&#x60; — with its client &#x60;id&#x60; and with &#x60;reviewed_at&#x60; set to the time of   the tap — to the IndexedDB store &#x60;kotobaya.outbox&#x60; (key: &#x60;id&#x60;; insertion order kept),   (2) shows the next held card at once, (3) sends the outbox in order, one request at a   time. On page load the outbox is sent first, in order; the server&#39;s per-&#x60;id&#x60;   idempotency makes a repeat safe (&#x60;200&#x60; with the stored review). Retryable failures —   no response, &#x60;429&#x60;, &#x60;5xx&#x60; — leave the entry in place and retry with backoff (1 s,   doubling, at most 30 s; at once when the browser reports online); the card is **not**   put back in front of the user and grading is never locked. Final refusals — &#x60;404&#x60;,   &#x60;409 card_not_reviewable&#x60;, &#x60;410 gone&#x60;, &#x60;422&#x60; — remove the entry and show one toast;   &#x60;401&#x60; stops sending and keeps the outbox until the user has logged in again. An   entry is removed only on a &#x60;2xx&#x60; or a final refusal. The client keeps at least 10   cards ahead (it fetches with &#x60;limit&#x3D;30&#x60; when fewer than 10 unanswered cards are   held), merges a fetched page into what it holds instead of discarding either, and   drops from a fetched page every card that has an entry in the outbox (the server has   not seen that answer yet and would serve the card again). Outbox entries are the   &#x60;pending&#x60; list of C7. Edge cases: two tabs share the store and may both send an   entry (harmless: same &#x60;id&#x60;; each removes it on its own &#x60;2xx&#x60;); an entry sent hours or   days after the tap is still accepted (the &#x60;reviewed_at&#x60; bounds do not depend on when   it arrives) and is logged at the time of the tap; an answer for a card deleted or   suspended elsewhere is the &#x60;410&#x60; / &#x60;409&#x60; above; clearing site data loses unsent   answers (the only way to lose one), so while the outbox is not empty and the browser   is offline the header shows an \&quot;n answers not saved yet\&quot; hint. - A client that sends &#x60;kinds&#x60; may get &#x60;reviewing&#x60; with empty &#x60;items&#x60; (the ready cards   are of a kind it excluded): for that filter there is nothing to show; &#x60;loop&#x60; still   describes the whole queue.  **Word audio (ADR-047, C-29).** Every item carries &#x60;word_audio&#x60;: for a &#x60;vocab&#x60; note the note&#39;s own audio when its &#x60;audio&#x60; field holds a &#x60;[sound:]&#x60;, else the generated speech of its reading in the effective voice, else null. For the first 20 served items whose speech is not generated yet the server queues it (urgent &#x60;tts&#x60; jobs within the urgent limit of &#x60;resolveSpeech&#x60; \&quot;Backlog\&quot;; a system-table write, no op; whatever the caller&#39;s scopes, since the texts come from the user&#39;s own notes), so the hash is usually there by the next fetch; a client with &#x60;write&#x60; that wants it sooner calls &#x60;resolveSpeech&#x60;. Nothing here reads or writes a note field, and no part of the queue (order, counts, &#x60;loop&#x60;) depends on audio.  **Keep going (C-26, ADR-031).** With &#x60;keep_going&#x3D;true&#x60; the builder runs with &#x60;QueueLimits.keepGoing &#x3D; true&#x60; and &#x60;keepGoingNewBatch &#x3D; limit&#x60; (core &#x60;QueueBuilder&#x60; rule 6): the daily limits (&#x60;scheduler.new_per_day&#x60;, &#x60;scheduler.reviews_per_day&#x60;, deck &#x60;new_cards_per_day&#x60; / &#x60;reviews_per_day&#x60;) are lifted, at most about &#x60;limit&#x60; new cards are drawn per request (a kanji group may overshoot), and the order is ready learning, then **all** due reviews (most overdue first), then new cards - no mix: with a review cap the backlog is cleared first, then new cards follow; with &#x60;scheduler.reviews_unlimited&#x60; new cards follow and a review that becomes due is slotted in next. Without &#x60;keep_going_new_target&#x60; the new cards follow without end (C-26); with it they stop when today&#39;s introductions reach the target (C-31, ADR-052: &#x60;QueueLimits.keepGoingNewTarget&#x60;, rule 6 \&quot;Target\&quot;; rule C2 says how a client chooses it). A learning card whose step has elapsed is always first. Everything else (eligibility, sibling burying, deck priority and order, disabled decks, learning) is unchanged. Cards not due before &#x60;day_ends_at&#x60; are never returned (no study-ahead). It is a stateless per-request flag: nothing is stored, no op is emitted, and the client keeps sending it on every fetch while the user keeps going. Cards answered this way are ordinary reviews and count against today&#39;s limits as usual. Everything in &#x60;loop&#x60; except &#x60;phase&#x60;, &#x60;keep_going&#x60;, &#x60;keep_going_available&#x60;, &#x60;until_stop&#x60; and &#x60;until_stop_parts&#x60; is the same with or without keep going (ADR-033 principle), so every client shows the same numbers at the same moment whatever mode it is in; so are the deprecated &#x60;counts&#x60;.  **No review limit (ADR-034).** If the setting &#x60;scheduler.reviews_unlimited&#x60; is &#x60;true&#x60; (SCHEMA.md 6.3; default &#x60;false&#x60;), every build (keep going or not) ignores &#x60;scheduler.reviews_per_day&#x60; and deck &#x60;reviews_per_day&#x60;: every review due before &#x60;day_ends_at&#x60; is returned and counted in &#x60;loop.ready.review&#x60; (&#x60;loop.reviews.limit&#x60; is null, &#x60;loop.backlog.beyond_limit&#x60; 0). The new goal and the proportional mix apply as usual; at the goal, once no learning card is left for today (ADR-049), the phase is &#x60;goal_reached&#x60;. It is an ordinary setting written with &#x60;updateSettings&#x60;.  **Reading cards retired (ADR-035).** While the setting &#x60;cards.reading_enabled&#x60; is not &#x60;true&#x60; (default &#x60;false&#x60;; SCHEMA.md 6.4), vocab &#x60;reading&#x60; cards are never returned, never counted in &#x60;counts&#x60;, never bury their siblings, and today&#39;s reviews of them use no limit (core &#x60;QueueInput.readingEnabled&#x60;, QueueBuilder rule 0). Materialized deck entries get no &#x60;reading&#x60; card. Setting it &#x60;true&#x60; returns the same cards with their history.  **No learn-ahead (ADR-033).** A learning or relearning card is returned only once its &#x60;due_at&#x60; has passed (core &#x60;QueueLimits.learnAheadMs&#x60; &#x3D; 0 on the server and on devices). Cards due later today are counted in &#x60;loop.later_today&#x60; and are only a number while a card can be shown under the limits (reviews, new cards left to the goal). When none can (ADR-049), the phase is &#x60;waiting&#x60;, also when the goal is reached and new cards remain: the client shows a waiting state (\&quot;next card in m:ss\&quot;, plus Keep going iff &#x60;loop.keep_going_available&#x60;) and fetches again at &#x60;loop.later_today.next_due_at&#x60; (rule C4). Waiting never serves a card early: a card is never shown again before its learning step has passed.  **Keep-going scope (normative).** Rule C2 above: one review visit of one client, held in memory, never persisted.  **Kanji first (ADR-040).** A new vocab card is never served before the new kanji cards of the kanji it requires. Those kanji cards come immediately before it, in the order of the word&#39;s kanji, as one **group**: the proportional mix never puts a review between them (only a learning card that falls due can come between; the group then continues right after it) and the whole group counts against the new-card goal. A group that does not fit today&#39;s remainder is not skipped and never overshoots the goal (ADR-043): as many of its kanji as fit are served today, and the word follows, behind its last kanji, on the first day the rest fits; nothing behind that word in its deck is introduced before it (a group may overshoot &#x60;limit&#x60;, never the goal). This holds for every word, whatever deck it comes from and for words in no deck; the deck setting &#x60;add_kanji&#x60; and the setting &#x60;scheduler.kanji_before_vocab&#x60; are no longer read. A kanji that already has a card in learning or review does not block and is not shown again; a suspended kanji card and a kanji note the user deleted are respected (the word comes without it); a kanji card the user **buried** holds its word back until the bury ends (ADR-043). A kanji card joins its word&#39;s group even when its own decks are all disabled. **Missing kanji notes are created here**, like deck entries (C-27): when the served build draws a word whose required kanji has no kanji note, the server creates the kanji note (&#x60;note.created&#x60; &#x60;origin&#x60; &#x60;auto_kanji&#x60;, built-in kanji type, and its default &#x60;card.created&#x60;s) in the same op transaction, before any vocab note of that fetch, and rebuilds; the word is withheld from &#x60;items&#x60; until its kanji exist. **Backfill:** the required kanji of words that are already being studied come first among the new cards, the oldest word&#39;s first: kanji cards that are still new, and (ADR-043) kanji that have no note yet, which are created in the same way. These kanji use at most half of the day&#39;s new goal (&#x60;floor(scheduler.new_per_day / 2)&#x60;, at least 1; ADR-044); the rest goes to new words with their own kanji, and when no new word can be drawn the kanji may use the remainder. Such a kanji card that is in no deck (every automatically created one) is drawn only through this share, never as an ordinary deckless new card; one that belongs to a deck is drawn through this share too (whatever the deck&#39;s priority or limit) and may also come up earlier as an ordinary card of its enabled deck. With &#x60;keep_going&#x3D;true&#x60; they are at most half of the new cards introduced in the study day. Example: goal 4, three such kanji and two new words → &#x60;日 本 うっかり Ｔシャツ&#x60; today, &#x60;語&#x60; on the next study day. Core &#x60;QueueBuilder&#x60; rule 4 \&quot;Kanji first\&quot; is normative. Example: new word 学校, no kanji notes, 3 reviews ready, 3 new left today → &#x60;R1 学 校 学校 R2 R3&#x60;.  **Which kanji a word requires (ADR-042, ADR-045, ADR-046).** Decided by the core predicate &#x60;KanjiRequirement&#x60; from the setting &#x60;cards.kanji_exemptions&#x60; (bool, default &#x60;true&#x60;; SCHEMA.md 6.5). While it is on: a word requires its **learnable** kanji only (jōyō, i.e. KANJIDIC grade 1-6 or 8, or KANJIDIC frequency rank 1-2500); a word whose displayed form is word-level tagged (JMdict &#x60;uk&#x60; on the sense used, or the kanji form tagged &#x60;ateji&#x60;, &#x60;rK&#x60;, &#x60;oK&#x60;, &#x60;iK&#x60; or &#x60;sK&#x60;) requires its **jōyō** kanji only (ADR-046). A word written with kanji shows its reading as furigana over the whole word on the front (&#x60;QueueItem.front_furigana&#x60;) when its displayed form is word-level tagged or when it requires no kanji. With the setting &#x60;false&#x60; every kanji is required and no front has furigana. A **kana word** (every kanji form of its entry is &#x60;rK&#x60; / &#x60;sK&#x60;; its &#x60;headword&#x60; is the reading) has no kanji either way. Examples: 寿司 (ateji) → 寿, 司 first, furigana front; 金糸雀 (ateji; 雀 is not jōyō) → 金, 糸 first, furigana front; 昨日 → 昨, 日 first, no furigana; 鱈 → no kanji card, furigana; シンガポール (forms 新嘉坡 … all &#x60;rK&#x60;) → front &#x60;シンガポール&#x60;, no kanji card.  **Old clients.** A client written before ADR-037 keeps working: &#x60;counts&#x60;, &#x60;more_available&#x60; and &#x60;next_learning_due_at&#x60; are still sent with their old meaning (see the schema). What it sees differently: the order (the proportional mix instead of the old interleave) and no per-session \&quot;done for now\&quot; (&#x60;scheduler.new_per_session&#x60; is ignored; &#x60;counts.new&#x60; is the day&#39;s remainder, never a session&#39;s).  **Deck entries become cards here (C-27, ADR-032).** Deck items &#x60;e:&lt;seq&gt;&#x60; with no note are new cards too (core &#x60;PendingEntry&#x60;; which entries qualify: contracts/core README \&quot;C-27\&quot;). The server passes them to the core builder, and when its build draws some (&#x60;toMaterialize&#x60; of the build that is **served** - &#x60;LoopResult.queue&#x60;; never the other build&#39;s - bounded by the same new-card budget as the cards: the day&#39;s remaining goal and the batch &#x60;limit&#x60;, or with keep going the batch alone; each entry counted as the whole kanji group its card will be, so an entry that would not fit is not drawn) and the caller holds &#x60;review&#x60; or &#x60;write&#x60;, the server creates them before answering, in one op transaction as the **server device**, exactly as contracts/core README \&quot;C-27\&quot; defines: first the kanji notes of the build&#39;s &#x60;kanjiToMaterialize&#x60; (ADR-040: the missing required kanji of every drawn word, whatever the deck&#39;s &#x60;add_kanji&#x60;; &#x60;note.created&#x60; &#x60;origin&#x60; &#x60;auto_kanji&#x60; + their &#x60;card.created&#x60;s; never a kanji the user deleted), then per drawn entry, in the builder&#39;s order, &#x60;note.created&#x60; {built-in vocab type, &#x60;entry_seq&#x60;, &#x60;fields&#x60; &#x60;{}&#x60;, &#x60;origin&#x60; &#x60;deck&#x60;} and one &#x60;card.created&#x60; per kind of the drawing deck&#39;s &#x60;card_kinds&#x60; (the vocab type&#39;s default kinds when that is absent, empty or invalid), minus kinds whose front would render empty. It then rebuilds and returns that queue; it materializes again (at most 3 rounds) only while the previous round&#39;s notes appear in &#x60;items&#x60;. This is the only place an API client&#39;s deck entries become notes: clients never mint these ops or call anything else for it; a device builds offline from the same core output and mints the same ops itself. - **Idempotent.** The transaction holds the sync lock and skips an entry a live note   already holds, so repeated or concurrent fetches never create a second note; a   fetch that draws nothing emits nothing (and takes no lock). - **Devices.** A device that created a note for the same entry offline converges by   SYNC.md 8.1 (the note that held the entry first survives; the other&#39;s cards and   reviews are merged into it). - **Read-only callers** (&#x60;read&#x60; only) get the queue without materializing: drawn   entries are counted in &#x60;to_materialize&#x60; (and the deprecated &#x60;counts.new&#x60;), not in   &#x60;items&#x60;; &#x60;loop.phase&#x60; is &#x60;reviewing&#x60; (rule C5). - &#x60;Kotobaya-Seq&#x60; is set when ops were emitted.  Each item carries everything needed to render and grade the card: the card with its note summary, the dictionary entry (when the note references one that exists), the example sentence chosen for rotating-example cards, the &#x60;review_type&#x60; an answer will be logged with, and a preview of the next due time per rating. Grammar cards also carry &#x60;grammar_sentence&#x60; (C-15): the rotated example sentence and span, chosen by the core picker with the rules on &#x60;GrammarCardSentence&#x60; (drill sessions use the same rules). 
         * @param limit Maximum number of cards to return. (optional, default to 20)
         * @param deckId Restrict new cards to this deck and reviews to cards whose note or entry is an item of it. Must be a live deck (&#x60;404&#x60; otherwise). (optional)
         * @param kinds Which queue sections to include, comma-separated. Default all three. (optional, default to setOf("new","learning","review"))
         * @param keepGoing C-26 (additive). &#x60;true&#x60; &#x3D; keep going past today&#39;s limits (see the description; core &#x60;QueueBuilder&#x60; rule 6, with &#x60;keepGoingNewBatch &#x3D; limit&#x60;). Default &#x60;false&#x60; &#x3D; the normal queue. Scope (ADR-039, rule C2 in the description): one review visit of one client, held in memory; every new visit, home screen and status fetch sends &#x60;false&#x60;. Per client, never synced. Only the literal values &#x60;true&#x60; and &#x60;false&#x60; are accepted; anything else is &#x60;422 validation_failed&#x60; with &#x60;errors[0].pointer &#x3D; /keep_going&#x60; and &#x60;code &#x3D; invalid_value&#x60;. Echoed as &#x60;loop.keep_going&#x60;. Older servers ignore the parameter (they return the normal queue and no &#x60;more_available&#x60;).  (optional, default to false)
         * @param keepGoingNewTarget C-31 (additive; ADR-051, ADR-052). The number of new cards introduced today (&#x60;loop.new.today&#x60;) up to which this keep-going request may introduce new cards: core &#x60;QueueLimits.keepGoingNewTarget&#x60;. Absent &#x3D; no target (the endless keep going of C-26). **Ignored unless &#x60;keep_going&#x3D;true&#x60;** (it is still validated), so a normal request never serves or counts a new card beyond today&#39;s goal. A value at or below &#x60;loop.new.today&#x60; (already reached, also &#x60;0&#x60;) introduces no new card: due learning cards and due reviews are still served, and when there are none the phase is &#x60;goal_reached&#x60; / &#x60;done_for_today&#x60; (new cards remain) or &#x60;empty&#x60;. Clients send &#x60;loop.new.today + max(1, loop.new.goal)&#x60; of the response on which Keep going was pressed and repeat that same value on every fetch until the next press (rule C2); they never persist it. Constraints: a decimal integer &#x60;0&#x60; … &#x60;2147483647&#x60;, no sign, no fraction; anything else is &#x60;422 validation_failed&#x60; with &#x60;errors[0].pointer &#x3D; /keep_going_new_target&#x60; and &#x60;code &#x3D; invalid_value&#x60; (not a number) or &#x60;out_of_range&#x60;. Not echoed in the response. A server older than C-31 answers &#x60;422&#x60; (&#x60;unknown_field&#x60;) to it; server and web deploy from one pin, and the shell declares support by &#x60;LOCAL_API_VERSION&#x60; 3. Examples: goal 40, 40 introduced, press → &#x60;80&#x60;; goal 0, 5 introduced → &#x60;6&#x60;.  (optional)
         * @return ReviewQueue
        */
            @Suppress("UNCHECKED_CAST")
        open suspend fun getReviewQueue(limit: kotlin.Int? = 20, deckId: kotlin.String? = null, kinds: kotlin.collections.Set<kotlin.String>? = setOf("new","learning","review"), keepGoing: kotlin.Boolean? = false, keepGoingNewTarget: kotlin.Int? = null): HttpResponse<ReviewQueue> {

            val localVariableAuthNames = listOf<String>("sessionCookie", "bearerAuth")

            val localVariableBody = 
                    io.ktor.client.utils.EmptyContent

            val localVariableQuery = mutableMapOf<String, List<String>>()
            limit?.apply { localVariableQuery["limit"] = listOf("$limit") }
            deckId?.apply { localVariableQuery["deck_id"] = listOf("$deckId") }
            kinds?.apply { localVariableQuery["kinds"] = toMultiValue(this, "csv") }
            keepGoing?.apply { localVariableQuery["keep_going"] = listOf("$keepGoing") }
            keepGoingNewTarget?.apply { localVariableQuery["keep_going_new_target"] = listOf("$keepGoingNewTarget") }

            val localVariableHeaders = mutableMapOf<String, String>()

            val localVariableConfig = RequestConfig<kotlin.Any?>(
            RequestMethod.GET,
            "/review/queue",
            query = localVariableQuery,
            headers = localVariableHeaders,
            requiresAuthentication = true,
            )

            return request(
            localVariableConfig,
            localVariableBody,
            localVariableAuthNames
            ).wrap()
            }

        /**
        * POST /review/undo
        * Undo a review.
        * Server emits &#x60;review.undone&#x60; for the review named by &#x60;review_id&#x60; and recomputes the card.  - &#x60;review_id&#x60; is **required** (any review, whichever device logged it, &#x60;manual&#x60; rows   included). There is no implicit \&quot;undo my last review\&quot;: that would not be idempotent   (a retry after a lost response would undo the review before it). The web app keeps the   &#x60;id&#x60; it sent to &#x60;POST /review/answer&#x60; and passes it here. - Target already undone: &#x60;200&#x60; with the current state, no op (an undo cannot be undone).   Hence retries are safe. - Unknown &#x60;review_id&#x60;: &#x60;404 not_found&#x60;. 
         * @param reviewUndoRequest  
         * @return ReviewResult
        */
            @Suppress("UNCHECKED_CAST")
        open suspend fun undoReview(reviewUndoRequest: ReviewUndoRequest): HttpResponse<ReviewResult> {

            val localVariableAuthNames = listOf<String>("sessionCookie", "bearerAuth")

            val localVariableBody = reviewUndoRequest

            val localVariableQuery = mutableMapOf<String, List<String>>()

            val localVariableHeaders = mutableMapOf<String, String>()

            val localVariableConfig = RequestConfig<kotlin.Any?>(
            RequestMethod.POST,
            "/review/undo",
            query = localVariableQuery,
            headers = localVariableHeaders,
            requiresAuthentication = true,
            )

            return jsonRequest(
            localVariableConfig,
            localVariableBody,
            localVariableAuthNames
            ).wrap()
            }

        }
